= Safety factor =

== Overview ==
A '''safety factor''' (factor of safety) is a design margin expressed as a ratio between a limiting/design condition and the maximum expected operating condition, with exact conventions depending on the discipline and standard.

== Definition ==
NASA safety terminology defines factor of safety as the ratio of the design condition to the maximum operating conditions specified during design.

== Scope & importance ==
Safety factors provide margin for uncertainty in loads, material properties, manufacturing variation, degradation, modeling assumptions, and operating conditions.

== Core concepts ==
A safety factor must be interpreted with the governing standard: what quantities form the numerator and denominator, which failure mode is considered, and whether additional partial factors or margins are used.

== Examples ==
If a component is qualified for a design load substantially above its allowed maximum operating load, the ratio expresses a reserve against the specified limit. The precise acceptable value depends on application and standard.

== Applications / implications ==
Safety factors are used in structural, mechanical, aerospace, civil, pressure-system, lifting, and other engineering disciplines.

== Epistemic notes ==
There is no universal safety factor appropriate to every system. Treating a single numerical factor as proof of safety can obscure failure modes, uncertainty, fatigue, reliability, quality control, and standard-specific requirements.

